[[:db/retract 17592186145166 :answer-field/choices 17592186145168] [:db/retract 17592186145166 :answer-field/choices 17592186145169] [:db/retract 17592186145166 :answer-field/choices 17592186145170] [:db/retract 17592186145166 :answer-field/choices 17592186145171] [:db/retract 17592186145166 :answer-field/choices 17592186145167] {:db/id 17592186147404 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-14f05b2e-14df-5ae0-910f-5581a3233e67" "question-f40e1a9a-bc30-5780-acaf-efe9da07376c" "question-efe88fd4-c2df-5ce1-afe8-3a63fe72cba4" "question-ecc4c2b5-f2f1-588a-9d0e-40046352c05f" "question-8347480c-1522-54bc-abef-f32d5cd4f309"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147168 :question/problem "What fraction is missing from the statement below?\n\n![](images/5b/5bc4e442148d5f0b64a16d9236ade1c1c9e962d1b922cc5e21d6cb7073eef45c.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is\n\n$$\n(15\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 13}}) \\times (4\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 5}}).\n$$\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 5}}$ and the ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 13}}$:\n\n$$\n(15\\, {\\color{red}{\\div\\, 5}}) \\times (4\\, {\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 13}})\n$$\n\nWe can now interpret the divisions as fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15} 5 \\times \\dfrac 4 {13}\n$$\n\nSo the missing fraction is $\\dfrac{15} 5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147405 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-aea04cef-b865-5601-90d6-5bec4382239a" "question-0a115e9c-bb91-5dd4-bd1d-ae911267b348" "question-fe9d7537-9d25-5842-8fc7-2ece42e6d933" "question-a80469df-5371-5771-a53c-6877f0cc2ac2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147169 :question/problem "Find an expression of the form ![](images/5d/5d571b53dea77754a9eccf1c33b3ca4224f8db534141773f385b3f5ae42a26b2.svg) equivalent to $\\dfrac{4}{3}\\times\\dfrac{9}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n(4\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}}) \\times (9\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 2}}).\n$$\n\nWe use parentheses to make it easier to read.\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}}$ and the ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\,2}}$:\n\n$$\n(4\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\,2}}) \\times (9\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 3}})\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the $4\\div 2$ and $9\\div 3$ as fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{2} \\times \\dfrac{9}{3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147406 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-36cbce10-7136-5dc9-8dd6-df6f370a71a6" "question-27a694ea-4a25-512f-a531-3c15a047f01c" "question-635b731f-24aa-5bbe-94a3-dad141942646" "question-4879e2d6-8cad-5506-bb76-0080ddea786f" "question-c902914b-5a1f-5bfc-bc60-074785c6db67"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147170 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac{12}{7}\\times \\dfrac{21}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(12\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the ${\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}}$ and ${\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}}$:\n\n$$\n(12\\,{\\color{blue}{\\div\\, 4}})\\times(21\\,{\\color{red}{\\div\\, 7}})\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{12}{4}\\times\\dfrac{21}{7}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{12}{4}}_{⏟}}_{3}\\times {{\\frac{21}{7}}_{⏟}}_{3}=3\\times 3=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147407 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-826ca900-49ec-524f-b02a-6573662e88a0" "question-fceaf763-2a1c-5ef1-b51c-0b12e59864a9" "question-4b8ecff1-169c-50b4-9995-e59b8594fed1" "question-681d41b4-1702-556b-95c8-796404cb03b3" "question-4761ec95-7832-59a1-a543-1121304509d3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147186 :question/problem "What is ![](images/ef/efa6dc23b03222213c112d946567ac1ee879bbfa007e1245f9ceedbf4f8c0dea.svg) expressed as a fraction in lowest terms?" :question/worked-solution "We need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n![](images/88/8889a8caf91247de656b139eac0ac1cda307a95304fa3f31c6b66b3b43b4c7ac.svg)\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator (![](images/b9/b992f271508479b55a84d515f24ad943e6d6cebee3cf8ca8eba23c53d2ffce30.svg)) and the second fraction's numerator (![](images/e6/e6c919a9391008f140a302a91592baadf4fda7df09436f355d97029a17a0452a.svg)) have a common \nfactor of ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg) \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n![](images/01/01248649d0990953974a79f89b48a1cc95c30f9a5d0614b31c38bacefec9f639.svg)\n\nNext, we reduce the second fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by ![](images/fe/fe34f715005da1a8b682c9650a76785673fdf25c3b7c7546bb94824470c54caf.svg) \n\n![](images/15/15c1044aca3df2ff8f02df2e860fefb8d7f640f9bfa333b16bb44d4689aa7361.svg)\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators and we multiply the denominators:\n![](images/7b/7bb4e93fe78704795ace2ead756ff9fc49da5fd9ec1479f2b44e823e37e1be6f.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-14f05b2e-14df-5ae0-910f-5581a3233e67" :question/id #uuid "14f05b2e-14df-5ae0-910f-5581a3233e67" :question/math-academy-id "q-136887" :question/problem "What fraction is missing from the statement below?\n\n$$\n(2÷9)\\times (8÷13)=\\frac{2}{13}\\times \\frac{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given expression is\n\n$$\n(2\\,÷\\,9)\\times (8\\,÷\\,13).\n$$\n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can write an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,9$ and the $÷\\,13:$\n\n$$\n(2\\,÷\\,13)\\times (8\\,÷\\,9)\n$$\n\nWe can now interpret the divisions as fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{13}\\times \\frac{8}{9}\n$$\n\nSo the missing fraction is $\\frac{8}{9}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7ee91810-62a9-50fe-b168-d229ad3a5fae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7ee91810-62a9-50fe-b168-d229ad3a5fae" :answer-field/id #uuid "7ee91810-62a9-50fe-b168-d229ad3a5fae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-38be066b-1060-556f-b0f3-3b2e5b8a2241" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c5be527c-e0e3-5fff-9dfe-951de67b43cb" "answer-7b3bd8d7-a532-536f-b660-f76e9b30185c" "answer-04459a90-5778-53c3-81a4-feacfd08716f" "answer-38be066b-1060-556f-b0f3-3b2e5b8a2241" "answer-d87dc4e3-59e2-52d3-b309-8abd9fb6f741"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5be527c-e0e3-5fff-9dfe-951de67b43cb" :answer/id #uuid "c5be527c-e0e3-5fff-9dfe-951de67b43cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b3bd8d7-a532-536f-b660-f76e9b30185c" :answer/id #uuid "7b3bd8d7-a532-536f-b660-f76e9b30185c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04459a90-5778-53c3-81a4-feacfd08716f" :answer/id #uuid "04459a90-5778-53c3-81a4-feacfd08716f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38be066b-1060-556f-b0f3-3b2e5b8a2241" :answer/id #uuid "38be066b-1060-556f-b0f3-3b2e5b8a2241" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d87dc4e3-59e2-52d3-b309-8abd9fb6f741" :answer/id #uuid "d87dc4e3-59e2-52d3-b309-8abd9fb6f741" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f40e1a9a-bc30-5780-acaf-efe9da07376c" :question/id #uuid "f40e1a9a-bc30-